18;tetramerization&#x02019; domain: implications for pore structure. <source>Nat. Struct. Biol.</source> <volume>6</volume>:<fpage>1122</fpage>&#x02013;1125.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10581553</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Kreusch, A., P.J. Pfaffinger, C.F. Stevens, and S. Choe. <year>1998</year>. Crystal structure of the tetramerization domain of the Shaker potassium channel. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>392</volume>:<fpage>945</fpage>&#x02013;948.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9582078</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Kuo, A., J.M. Gulbis, J.F. Antcliff, T. Rahman, E.D. Lowe, J. Zimmer, J. Cuthbertson, F.M. Ashcroft, T. Ezaki, and D.A. Doyle. <year>2003</year>. Crystal structure of the potassium channel KirBac1.1 in the closed state. <source>Science.</source> <volume>300</volume>:<fpage>1922</fpage>&#x02013;1926.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12738871</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal">Lopez, G.A., Y.N. Jan, and L.Y. Jan. <year>1994</year>. Evidence that the S6 segment of the Shaker voltage-gated K<sup>+</sup> channel comprises part of the pore. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>367</volume>:<fpage>179</fpage>&#x02013;182.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8114915</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation citation-type="journal">Lu, Z., A.M. Klem, and Y. Ramu. <year>2001</year>. Ion conduction pore is conserved among potassium channels. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>413</volume>:<fpage>809</fpage>&#x02013;813.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11677598</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="journal">MacKinnon, R. <year>2003</year>. Potassium channels. <source>FEBS Lett.</source> <volume>555</volume>:<fpage>62</fpage>&#x02013;65.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14630320</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><citation citation-type="journal">MacKinnon, R., S.L. Cohen, A. Kuo, A. Lee, and B.T. Chait. <year>1998</year>. Structura